{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And whatever of them falls upon it when they die, it shall be impure, of every wooden vessel</b> — It seems to me in this matter that \"of them when they die\" alludes to all those mentioned above — unclean animal, creeping thing, and beast — for why would Scripture mention the impurity of vessels and garments specifically in connection with the creeping things, when it applies also to carcasses? And likewise, all the rulings that Scripture mentions regarding the permissibility of foods are all equal in all impurities, and the verse speaks of them inclusively. And do not find it difficult that the first verse has \"in them when they die\" regarding the creeping things, while the second verse has \"of them when they die\" regarding all of them, for so it is in many places. And it is possible to say that Scripture waited until it had mentioned all the impure creatures among the animals, beasts, and creeping things, and afterward, at the end, it mentioned the impurity of vessels in connection with one of them, through which it completes the teaching for all the others. For the opinion of our Sages (Ḥagigah 11a) is that \"of them when they die\" refers to the creeping things, and from this they learn about the creeping thing that it imparts impurity at the size of a lentil. And so Scripture did with immersion: it mentioned (in this verse) at the end of all the impurities the impurity of vessels and their immersion, and from this we learn for a person and for vessels in all the other impurities mentioned." "<b>It shall be brought into water</b> — And even after its immersion it remains impure for terumah until sunset, and afterward it becomes pure at sunset. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 11:32). And its midrash in Torat Kohanim (chapter 8, mishnah 9): \"'And it is impure until evening' — one might think for everything? Scripture says: 'and it becomes pure.' If 'and it becomes pure' — one might think for everything? Scripture says: 'and it is impure until evening.' How so? It is pure for ḥullin before nightfall, and for terumah from when it grows dark.\" And the reading of the verse according to the midrash is: it shall be brought into water and it becomes pure and it is impure until evening. And according to the plain sense: it shall be brought into water and it is impure until evening and it becomes pure at evening. And Scripture mentioned \"until evening\" in all the sections, for purification takes effect for terumah of consecrated things, as it says (below 22:6–7): \"And he is impure until evening and he shall not eat of the consecrated things unless he washes his flesh in water. And when the sun sets he becomes pure and afterward he may eat of the consecrated things, for it is his bread.\" But purification for ḥullin or for tithes is through immersion alone, and Scripture does not mention it here, for one is not commanded to purify oneself for ḥullin, and if he wished, he may eat them in impurity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The verse reference חגיגה י\"א is given in the format (page side omitted); resolved to Ḥagigah 11a per standard Talmudic citation. The phrase 'בכעדשה' rendered 'at the size of a lentil.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:03.652020697Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}